IMPORT prelude

-- Health Products Act 2007 (Singapore) -- supplying health products: what "supply" covers (s 2), unregistered products (s 15),
-- adulterated, counterfeit, tampered and unwholesome products and the due-diligence defence (s 16), false advertising and the
-- publisher's defence (ss 20, 22), active ingredients (s 47), and cosmetics (First Schedule).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/HPA2007.txt (First Schedule as amended to
-- S 103/2021).
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 13 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository. SCOPED to the offences a seller or advertiser
-- meets. Not encoded: manufacture and import licensing, wholesaling, licences and registration procedure, post-market duties, the
-- enforcement powers, and the regulations (which fix the categories' detailed requirements; not retrieved).
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Health Products Act 2007 -- supply`

§§ `2 -- supply`

-- s 2: "supply" means "to transfer possession of the health product by any means WHETHER OR NOT FOR REWARD", including selling, and
-- "to expose or display the health product as an invitation to treat".
DECLARE Transfer IS ONE OF
    `selling online`
    `giving away free samples`
    `displaying on a shop shelf`
    `keeping for personal use`

GIVEN `the transfer` IS A Transfer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the transfer is a supply` IF
    CONSIDER `the transfer`
    WHEN `keeping for personal use` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `15, First Schedule -- registration`

-- s 15(1): "Except in such cases as may be prescribed", a person must not supply a health product unless it is registered. (2): $50,000
-- or 2 years.
-- First Schedule: for a COSMETIC PRODUCT, "Sections 12(1), (2) and (3), 13(1), (2) and (3), 14(1) and (2), 15 and 19(1)(b), and
-- Parts 6 and 7 do not apply" -- so cosmetics need no registration; s 16 (adulteration, counterfeits) still applies.
DECLARE Product IS ONE OF
    `a therapeutic product`
    `a medical device`
    `a cosmetic product`

GIVEN `the product` IS A Product, `registered` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `supplying it is a section 15 offence` IF
        (NOT `registered`)
    AND CONSIDER `the product`
        WHEN `a cosmetic product` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `16 -- adulterated and counterfeit products`

-- s 16(1): no one may supply, or procure or arrange the supply of, a health product that is (a) adulterated, (b) counterfeit, (c)
-- tampered with, or (d) unwholesome. (2): (a)-(c) -- $100,000 or 3 years; (d) -- $50,000 or 2 years.
-- (3): defence -- the accused did not know, had no reason to believe, AND could not with reasonable diligence have ascertained, AND took
-- all reasonable precautions and due diligence.
DECLARE Defect IS ONE OF
    `adulterated`
    `counterfeit`
    `tampered with`
    `unwholesome`

GIVEN `the defect` IS A Defect
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in years` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the defect`
    WHEN `unwholesome` THEN 2
    OTHERWISE 3

GIVEN `did not know and had no reason to believe` IS A BOOLEAN, `could not have found out with reasonable diligence` IS A BOOLEAN, `took all reasonable precautions` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the seller has the section 16 defence` IF
    `did not know and had no reason to believe` AND `could not have found out with reasonable diligence` AND `took all reasonable precautions`

§§ `20, 22 -- advertising`

-- s 20(2): an advertisement is false or misleading if it falsely describes the product or "is likely to create an erroneous impression
-- regarding the formulation, composition, design specification, quality, safety, efficacy or uses". (3): $20,000 or 12 months.
-- s 22: a defence for a person whose BUSINESS is publishing advertisements, who received it in the ordinary course, has NO FINANCIAL
-- INTEREST in the product's supply, and did not know and had no reason to suspect. An influencer paid by the brand has a financial
-- interest and no defence.
DECLARE Publisher HAS
    `publishes ads as a business` IS A BOOLEAN
    `received it in the ordinary course` IS A BOOLEAN
    `has a financial interest in the product` IS A BOOLEAN
    `knew or suspected` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the publisher` IS A Publisher
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the publisher has the section 22 defence` IF
        `the publisher`'s `publishes ads as a business`
    AND `the publisher`'s `received it in the ordinary course`
    AND (NOT `the publisher`'s `has a financial interest in the product`)
    AND (NOT `the publisher`'s `knew or suspected`)

§§ `47 -- active ingredients`

-- s 47(4): contravening the active-ingredient regulations -- $50,000 or 2 years. (5): a person proved to have kept or possessed an active
-- ingredient "is presumed to have done so KNOWINGLY unless the contrary is proved".
GIVEN `possessed the ingredient` IS A BOOLEAN, `proved lack of knowledge` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `knowing possession is established` IF
    `possessed the ingredient` AND (NOT `proved lack of knowledge`)
